{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ba6a68d6-c702-4382-ab2f-60cd0f6af675","hash":"12fb55ca1d5b7d59bdab65c51cdf1bf6935ebbfa0da5526cb129a100249da244","message":{"role":"system","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note: /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/ba6a68d6/tmp/pr-flake.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t`a_signal_restores_the_terminal_mode_the_session_changed` failed about every other run on macOS under parallel load: `left: None, right: Some(0)`, so a signal killed the process instead of watch handling it.\n2\t\n3\tThe test sent SIGTERM once the terminal was in raw mode. The keyboard thread sets raw mode before watchexec installs its signal handlers, so under load SIGTERM could arrive first and take the default action. The test now also waits until watch reports a file change, as the non-terminal signal tests already do, which proves the handlers are up.\n4\t\n5\t## Verify\n6\t\n7\t- `cargo nextest run -p caliper-docgen`: 6 consecutive full runs, 148 of 148 pass each time (it failed in about half before).\n8\t\n\n<total_tokens>14954960 tokens left</total_tokens>\n\nConcise output style is active. Be concise: lead with the result, skip preamble and narration, keep only what the user needs."}]}}
