{"parentUuid":"96ffa477-8362-4f44-944b-f7b28c13ff6b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d29d37d1-42a9-4a91-986a-e0277a3cbad8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Pkm2AGzreeoaZmpNRBXQb","type":"tool_result","content":"--- [user] 2026-10-23T05:50:03.406Z\n- **Storybook with the docgen server off, plus the Vite plugin:** a real `storybook build` bundled docs for all five components: a named export, a default export, a forwardRef, and two components in one file.\n\n## Measurements against React's docgen (react-component-meta)\n\n| | caliper | react-component-meta |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| First request | 33–43 ms | 504 ms |\n| Later requests (average of 20) | 0.36–0.60 ms | 0.65–1.47 ms |\n| Request after an edit | 3.9 ms, edit included | edit not seen (it relies on Storybook's file tracking) |\n\n- **argTypes match:** 19 of 26 are identical. Five of the seven differences are string defaults, where caliper prints `\"md\"` and React prints the source text `'md'`. The other two are types React's checker prints differently: `MouseEvent&lt;HTMLButtonElement, MouseEvent&gt;`, and optional fields with `| undefined` added.\n- **Cost of the per-request file check:** about 52 ms for 8,800 files.\n\n## Open issues\n- **Quote style of string defaults** is the main remaining mismatch with React's docgen. caliper's extractor always rewrites string defaults with double quotes; keeping the source text would change caliper's main output format, so I left it.\n- **Extractor bug:** a JSDoc comment on a one-line `function X(props: { a: string; … })` gets attached to the first prop instead of the component.\n- **Render-prop text:** a function prop with one parameter that returns a ReactNode is printed as `=&gt; void`.\n- **Index-file matching** works only when exactly one component has that export name. An anonymous default export can't receive docs in the Vite plu\n\n--- [assistant] 2026-10-23T05:50:30.519Z\nanything it can't document to React's docgen (react-component-meta).\n- **#119:** an `injectDocgenInfo` mode in the Vite plugin for Storybook 10 (or 11 with the docgen server off), with hot reload when a shared types file changes.\n- **#120:** removes `--format storybook`.\n\n**Verified with real Storybook builds** in both modes, against react-component-meta:\n\n| | caliper | react-component-meta |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| First request | 33–43 ms | 504 ms |\n| Later requests | 0.4–0.6 ms | 0.7–1.5 ms |\n| After an edit | 3.9 ms, edit included | edit not seen |\n\n19 of 26 argTypes are identical. Five of the seven differences are string-default quote style: we print `\"md\"`, React prints the source text `'md'`.\n\n**Decision for you:** should string defaults keep their source text (`'md'`)? That matches Storybook's React docgen but changes our canonical output. Default is to leave them as they are.\n\n**Now running:** a bug batch stacked on #109:\n1. A JSDoc comment on a one-line function is attached to the first prop instead of the component.\n2. Render props print as `=> void`.\n3. recharts' event handlers overwrite the DOM ones.\n4. `z.infer<typeof schema>` is unwrapped one level too far.\n\n**Open PRs, for merging:**\n- Main stack: `#97 ← #99 ← #101 ← #103 ← #104 ← #105 ← #106 ← #107 ← #109`\n- Track A: `#110 ← #112 ← #113 ← #115`\n- Track C: `#111 ← #114 ← #116 ← #118`\n- Track B: `#108 ← #117 ← #119 ← #120`\n- Independent: #98, #100, #102 (then `@dependabot rebase` on #84)\n\n--- [user] 2026-10-23T06:25:50.946Z\n\n- **Fix:** intersections now combine both types. A prop is required if any member requires it, and lists every declaration. When React's own types are loaded, the element's real attribute declaration also joins the intersection. `extends` still lets the derived declaration win.\n- **Still not fixed in curated mode.** This is the CLI default and what the Vite plugin and Storybook use. Only Full mode loads React's own type declarations, and the dependency loader skips them by design (ADR 0010). So in curated mode, `ChartLegendContent.onTimeUpdate` still shows only recharts' type. In Full mode with dependencies it now shows both types intersected. Fixing curated mode would mean loading React's attribute declarations there too, which needs an ADR 0010 amendment.\n- **Effect with default flags:** shadcn 1 prop (`Callout.variant` is now `\"default\"`, matching TypeScript), antd 2, spectrum 3; the rest are identical.\n- **Effect with `--load-dependencies`:** chakra 363 components, but only the `asChild` declarations list changes (it now lists both `PolymorphicProps`). shadcn 10, antd 3, spectrum 3.\n- **Speed:** chakra with dependencies went from 452 to 465 ms; the default run is unchanged. No snapshots changed.\n\n**4. `z.infer` (#123)**\n- **Cause:** #104 keeps an alias's name when its expansion can't be evaluated, but an indexed access that can't be evaluated (`T[\"_output\"]`) didn't count. Two related gaps:\n  - **Declared-type case:** an indexed access on a type passed as an argument (`z.infer&lt;Parsed&gt;`) never evaluated, even though it can.\n  - **Flags-off output:** it printed `z.\n\n--- [assistant] 2026-10-23T06:26:02.258Z\n-line function it used to land on the first prop. The old 120-byte window also turned license headers into component descriptions. This changes a few descriptions per corpus (mantine 11, antd 17, spectrum 27); every change I checked is an improvement.\n- **#122, merging props across an intersection:** when two members of an intersection declare the same prop, its type is now the intersection of both, confirmed against TypeScript's checker on shadcn. Before, one silently replaced the other. `extends` still lets the derived declaration win.\n  - **Limitation:** in the default curated mode (the CLI, Vite plugin and Storybook), recharts' `onTimeUpdate` still shows only recharts' type. React's own attribute declarations are only loaded in Full mode, and ADR 0010 skips them on purpose. Fixing that needs an amendment to that ADR.\n- **#